// check: refuse E_InitPerformsOwnOp
// A handler's state init performs an op that handler alone answers. The
// init runs where the install is made, before the install exists — by
// construction, for every install of it — so nothing can answer the perform,
// and the judgment refuses it at the declaration with nothing emitted,
// naming the perform in its chain. On pin c3410610ce41 this compiled to
// 38467 bytes of WAT and trapped at 134; the runtime's guard named the op,
// the handler and the fix, but only after a module existed.
effect F { g() -> Int }
handler hf with s = g() {
  g() => resume(42),
}
fn main(q) = (g()) ~> hf
